  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAn initial slug of rain and storms (including a severe storm in\nErie, PA) developed a bit earlier than expected and moved across\nthe area largely before 10 AM this morning, adding some extra\nuncertainty to the evolution over the next few hours. \n\nAs of 10:30 AM, additional convection is developing from central\nLake Erie back into parts of Northwest and West Central OH in\nthe lingering warm advection regime. While heating will be more\nlimited due to the first round of convection this morning, slow\ndestabilization is expected through early this afternoon as \nthese additional storms move across the area. Strong shear (bulk\nshear of 40-50kt+ and effective storm relative helicity of \n200-300 m2/s2) will be in place through early this afternoon, so\nif we are able to see sufficient destabilization the potential \nfor organized/rotating convection remains across eastern OH and \ninto western PA from around midday and into the early afternoon.\nWhile heating will be limited which adds uncertainty, we will \nneed to monitor for a damaging wind and perhaps tornado \npotential from around the I-77 corridor points east if we can \nsee any supercells or bowing line segments evolve in the \nstrongly sheared environment before 2 or 3 PM. \n\nWe should then see a brief break in convection during the mid to\nlate afternoon hours before the cold front approaches and\ncrosses this evening, mainly between 5-10 PM. Shear remains \nstrong enough for organized convection and a mixed mode of cells\nand small/potentially bowing line segments this evening, but\nconfidence in the airmass recovering behind earlier convection\nis on the lower side. This may result in a risk for damaging \nwinds and a localized tornado risk, evident by the the 5% \ntornado probabilities in the latest SPC SWODY1 outlook. These \nstorms appear to be driven by mesoscale processes mainly below \n700 mb, evident by an abundance of dry air aloft, and thus, \nanticipating this second round of convection to be fairly \nshallow, similar to the type of storms seen across Wisconsin \nyesterday (7/20). Confidence on this second round remains on the\nlower side, especially with regards to intensity and coverage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA strong cold front will move across the area tonight, ushering\nin a chilly air mass into the region, characterized by 850 mb\ntemperatures falling as low as 6C on Wednesday. Lake-enhanced\nrain showers will be possible throughout Wednesday across \nportions of Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania in \naddition to ample cloud cover, especially Wednesday morning and \nearly afternoon. As a result, high temperatures may struggle to \nget out of the 60s in some spots. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nA large upper-level ridge across the Western and Central CONUS\nwill gradually expand further east into the Great Lakes by\nFriday into the weekend, resulting in temperatures to return\nnear or slightly above normal in the 80s. However, a series of\nupper-level troughs across Hudson Bay appear poised to block the\neastward progression of the ridge. This pattern would appear to\nfavor increasing shower and thunderstorm chances across the area\nby early next week if trends continue.",
